{:pages-and-blocks
 [{:page {:block/title "015-advice"},
   :blocks
   [{:block/title
     "To love someone long-term is to attend a thousand funerals of the people they used to be. The people they’re too exhausted to be any longer.\nThe people they don’t recognise inside themselves anymore. The people they grew out of, the people they never ended up growing into.\nWe so badly want the people we love to get their spark back when it burns out; to become speedily found when they are lost. \nBut it is not our job to hold anyone accountable to the people they used to be. It is our job to travel with them between each version and to honour what emerges along the way. \nSometimes it will be an even more luminescent flame. Sometimes it will be a flicker that disappears and temporarily floods the room with a perfect and necessary darkness. —Heidi Priebe",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:user.property/epigraph_display-KSB3dHhj "yes"
                        :logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}]}
  {:page {:block/title "018-free-will"},
   :blocks
   [{:block/title
     "He says: All of our behavior can be traced to biological events about which we have no conscious knowledge: this has always suggested that free will is an illusion. —The Moral Landscape",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}
    {:block/title
     "The intention to do one thing and not another does not originate in consciousness-rather, it *appears* in consciousness, as does any thought or impulse that might oppose it. — Free Will, Sam Harris",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}
    {:block/title
     "SnowBorn1 says: I think quantum mechanics, double slit experiment... etc, shows some uncertainty. In determinism everything is certain, but if there is uncertainty it opens the door for free will. @SnowBorn1",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}
    {:block/title
     "Cindy Rays writes: I am not sure about free will. As I get older I see that a lot of what I do is governed by pre existing patterns or habits. On the other hand, I go to therapy every month and work on having different thought patterns and responses than my habitual thoughts and responses—that certainly seems like free will.\nMaybe the answer is just that there is less free will operating in my life than I might have thought. @cindyray2817",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}
    {:block/title
     "Russ R. Writes: Absolutely. The human body may be driven by \"machines\" as you say, but those machines are constantly being tweaked, in my opinion. The tweaking are the decisions we make that then become machines. However I believe those machines are what we might also call habits, and we definitely are choosing which habits to have. Some habits drive us (addictions) that we have given over control. But through hard work we can still change our response. They say an alcoholic is always an alcoholic that is making the choice to not drink.",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}
    {:block/title
     "Josh R says: I honestly have no idea, I feel like both arguments are possible.  I look at it as if I myself make decisions after thinking them through and weighing the options.  But those decisions are also based off of electrical signals and/or chemicals in my brain, and my brain basically just responds to things how it’s been trained. Any decision I make is based off of emotions and what I think is best....  So am I actually making the decisions I think I am or am I just following what my brain is saying to do and thinking I’m fully making decisions? I’m going to say I’m leaning towards having free will but I don’t think it’s an ultimate free will, I think it’s just that our brains out so complex that we are able to recalculate things as they happen and change quickly as well as over time.",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}
    {:block/title
     "[I know a woman who would say:] \"I do not believe in free will, but I act as though it exists, [but] I treat *others* as if it does not.” Meaning, [this woman] wants to act as though she can make choices, but she will treat others as though they don’t have free will, and therefore she has more compassion, or understanding, or at least [gives others the] benefit of the doubt — [because] they can’t control how they act. — Mike Maughan, No Stupid Questions",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}]}
  {:page {:block/title "019-animal-needs"},
   :blocks
   [{:block/title
     "here is an exherb from Leopold's book describing his concept of a \"land ethic\": The land ethic simply enlarges the boundaries of the community to include soils, waters, plants, and animals, or collectively: the land. This sounds simple: do we not already sing our love for and obligation to the land of the free and the home of the brave? Yes, but just what and whom do we love? Certainly not the soil, which we are sending helter-skelter down river. Certainly not the waters, which we assume have no function except to turn turbines, float barges, and carry off sewage. Certainly not the plants, of which we exterminate whole communities without batting an eye. Certainly not the animals, of which we have already extirpated many of the largest and most beautiful species. A land ethic of course cannot prevent the alteration, management, and use of these 'resources,' but it does affirm their right to continued existence, and, at least in spots, their continued existence in a natural state. In short, a land ethic changes the role of Homo sapiens from conqueror of the land-community to plain member and citizen of it. It implies respect for his fellow-members, and also respect for the community as such.",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}]}
  {:page {:block/title "021-skeptisism"},
   :blocks
   [{:block/title
     "Cindy R says: I think you need at least a baseline amount of skepticism, to protect you from the scams and conspiracy theories of the world. But you also need to figure out when is the time to set skepticism aside and trust in things. It's ironic that people who have no skepticism believe all of the conspiracy theories, but then people with too much skepticism believe in a different set of problematic things, like vaccines are bad or scientific research isn't legitimate. Clearly there's a need for moderation and balance.",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}
    {:block/title
     "Josh R says: I think you should be a medium amount of skeptical.  Because if you aren’t at all [skeptical] then you’ll basically believe anything, but if you’re too skeptical you’ll believe nothing, to the point that ([as with] conspiracy theories) you’ll  [believe anything].  I personally feel like I’m too skeptical by default and have gotten more as I’ve gotten older, but I’m pretty aware of it and try to not let it run wild.  Because it’s exciting to get caught up in ideas (like conspiracy theories) that coooould be true, but it’s really unhealthy to do that.... That isn’t really even helpful, because you have to be able to exist in said society, and hopefully be able to be happy with it.",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}]}
