  {:page {:block/title "2024 Christmas Book Presentation"},
   :blocks
   [{:block/title
     "Until we _know_ we are **_wrong_**, being **_wrong_** feels exactly like being ==**_wrong_**==. — David McRaney, *How Minds Change* (summarizing Kathryn Schulz)",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:user.property/epigraph_display-KSB3dHhj "yes" 
                        :logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}
    {:block/title
     "I will argue..., that questions about values—about meaning, morality, and life's larger purpose—are really questions about the well-being of conscious creatures. [click] Values, therefore, translate into facts that can be scientifically understood.",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}
    {:block/title
     "One of my critics put the concern this way: \"Morals are relative to the time and place in which they appear. If you do not already accept well-being as a value, then there seems to be no argument for why one should promote well-being.\" As proof of this assertion, he observed that I would be unable to convince the Taliban that they value the wrong things. By this standard, however, the truths of science are also \"relative to the time and place in which they appear,\" and there is no way to convince someone who does not value empirical evidence that he should value it. Despite 150 years of working at it, we still can't convince a majority of Americans that evolution is a fact. Does this mean biology isn't a proper science?",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}
    {:block/title
     "Without potential consequences at the level of experience—happiness, suffering, joy, despair, etc.—all talk of value is empty. Therefore, to say that an act is morally necessary, or evil, or blameless, is to make (tacit) claims about its consequences in the lives of conscious creatures.",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}
    {:block/title
     "I will argue, however, that questions about values—about meaning, morality, and life's larger purpose—are really questions about the well-being of conscious creatures. Values, therefore, translate into facts that can be scientifically understood.",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties
     {:user.property/author-rMuY3sVf "[[Harris, Sam]]",
      :logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}
    {:block/title
     "[W] here does conscious reasoning come into the picture? It is an attempt to justify the choice after it has been made. And it is, after all, the only way we have to try to explain to other people why we made a particular decision. But given our lack of access to the brain processes involved, our justification is often spurious: a post-hoc rationalization, or even a confabulation—a “story” born of the confusion between imagination and memory. #quote #card",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}]}
