  {:page {:build/journal 20250214},
   :blocks
   [{:block/title
     "Here is the information that will help you prepare for the coding challenge. I will schedule it according to your availability. Have a great day!\nTo prepare, we recommend brushing up on your ability to design and implement solutions for open-ended problems. This interview will involve live coding, on CoderPad. The focus will be on designing a clean and logical solution, writing efficient and readable code, and demonstrating your debugging skills. Expect to discuss your decisions, such as why you chose certain data structures or APIs, as we value understanding the reasoning behind your approach. While the problem is open-ended with no single \"correct\" answer, it’s helpful to think about scalability, usability, and error handling in your design. A solid grasp of parsing strings, working with collections (e.g., maps, lists), and basic algorithmic complexity will also be beneficial. Most importantly, stay calm, think aloud, and don’t hesitate to ask clarifying questions!Remind\nyou that this is an open ended problem, there’s no right or wrong answer, and that I’ll be asking questions about their approach throughout the interview.\nI would focus on [tree](((67ae8595-74d2-460f-9dc6-059f8e88e01b))), parser, big O, [array](((67ae8595-982c-455e-90bd-765fea9cc515))), [linked list](((67ae8595-8c84-4785-ac65-ac20f5da540b))) questions. However, the interviewers may change the questions, but they should all be focused on CS fundamentals, algorithms and data structures type of preparation.\nPlease be sure you think out loud, ask clarifying questions, and respond to \"hints\".",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties {:logseq.property.node/display-type :quote}}]}
  {:page {:build/journal 20250330},
   :blocks
   [{:block/title
     "I call it my billion-dollar mistake. At that time, I was designing the first comprehensive type system for references in an object-oriented language. My goal was to ensure that all use of references should be absolutely safe, with checking performed automatically by the compiler. But I couldn’t resist the temptation to put in a null reference, simply because it was so easy to implement. This has led to innumerable errors, vulnerabilities, and system crashes, which have probably caused a billion dollars of pain and damage in the last forty years. #quote",
     :build/tags #{:logseq.class/Quote-block},
     :build/properties
     {:user.property/source-XNvKZqQN
      "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch06-01-defining-an-enum.html?highlight=optional#the-option-enum-and-its-advantages-over-null-values",
